  "literary_context": "Exodus 1–15 moves through a deliberate sequence: oppression in Egypt, Moses’ call, repeated confrontations with Pharaoh, the plagues, the Passover meal, departure, the sea crossing, and a song celebrating deliverance. The sequence shifts from private suffering to a public escape and then to communal memory in song. Later biblical texts retell the event in settings that emphasize instruction, worship, or renewed hope.",
  "covenantal_significance": "The Exodus is not presented as escape with no further purpose. The people leave slavery to belong to the God who rescued them, and later instructions ground Israel’s obedience in that prior act. Deuteronomy 6, for example, tells parents to explain the commands by recalling that the LORD brought Israel out of Egypt. Rescue becomes the foundation for a shared covenant life.",
  "authorship_positions": [],
  "date_ranges": [],
  "original_audience": "",
  "historical_setting": "",
  "canonical_role": "The Exodus becomes a central pattern in Scripture for remembering rescue and imagining renewal. Deuteronomy and the Psalms retell it to teach later generations; Isaiah can use exodus language to speak of future deliverance. Paul and Hebrews also draw on the story when explaining faith and life in Christ. These later uses build on the Exodus narrative while applying it in their own contexts.",
